  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1234 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nMain concern for the near-term revolves around frost potential\nacross the north late tonight into Wednesday morning as clearing\noccurs and temperatures fall into the mid 30s. Though initial\nthoughts point toward more of a patchy frost setup for mainly  \nprotected and low lying areas, high pressure sitting overhead and \nlow dewpoint depressions would nudge toward headline considerations.\nHave additionally decreased low temperatures using NBM 10th \npercentile given favorable setup.\n\nPersistence forecast holds into the weekend as Hudson Bay high\ndominates the eastern CONUS. Ensembles then continue to bring \nmarginal rain chances to northeast Wisconsin this weekend as a \nsurface low spins up over the Intermountain West and ejects north \ninto Canada. However, current thinking is that high pressure will \nwin the battle as it so often does in this scenario, especially \nwith no veritable moisture transport and lack of low-level forcing\nmechanism. Have retained 20 to 40% PoPs over our western counties\nwhere confidence is higher in at least light rain occurring \nbefore scattering out further east.\n\nThough beyond the scope of this forecast period, better chances \nfor more widespread rainfall look to be during the middle of next \nweek as high pressure breaks down and deepening low pressure \ntracks through the upper Midwest. However, trends will have to be\nmonitored as ensembles still diverge on the overall synoptic\npattern. Otherwise, most of the extended will be characterized by\ntemperatures mainly in the 60s, warming into the 70s by early \nnext week.",
